.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt ROUTE 8
.Os ELSEWHERE
.Sh NAME
.Nm route
.Nd queue and dispatcher of job cards
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm route
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Cm add Ar file
.Nm route
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Cm hold Ar job
.Nm route
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Cm release Ar job
.Nm route
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Cm status
.Nm route
.Op Fl d Ar dir
.Cm serve Ar seat
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
keeps a queue of job cards, as
.Xr jobcard 5
defines them, and hands each card to the one seat it names.
It decides scope and custody, never acceptance.
It is not a daemon and opens no listener:
.Xr sshd 8
is the transport.
Each seat reaches
.Nm
through its own forced-command key, whose command is
.Ql route serve Ar seat ,
so a seat is known by its channel, never by what it says in a request.
The operator runs the other verbs on the build host as the router's user.
.Pp
The state is one directory of stage directories, an append-only ledger and
a lock; see
.Sx FILES .
A job is a directory named by its
.Cm job
field.
It lives in exactly one stage directory, and the stage directories are the
truth.
Each transition commits with one
.Xr rename 2
of the job's directory from one stage directory to another, and then
appends one line to the ledger, which is the history.
Every transition, and
.Cm status ,
holds an exclusive
.Xr flock 2
on the lock, so concurrent invocations are serialised and
.Cm status
never sees a job between two stages.
.Pp
The option is:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d Ar dir
Use the state directory
.Ar dir
instead of
.Pa /var/route .
It is meant for tests.
.El
.Pp
A
.Ar job
operand, from the command line or from a request, is refused (exit 1)
unless it matches the job alphabet of
.Xr jobcard 5 ,
.Ql [A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9-]{0,63} .
A
.Ar seat
operand must match
.Ql [a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31} ,
or
.Nm
exits 2.
Both are checked before any path is built from them.
.Pp
The verbs are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm add Ar file
Read
.Ar file ,
which must be at most 65536 bytes, and read
.Pa /etc/route.conf ,
also at most 65536 bytes, each exactly once.
Copy both into a new private staging directory
.Pa tmp/add.XXXXXXXXXX
and run
.Ql card -c tmp/add.XXXXXXXXXX/conf tmp/add.XXXXXXXXXX/card
with an empty environment.
Unless
.Xr card 1
exits 0 the card is refused.
So the bytes judged are the bytes kept, and the configuration judged is the
one used.
.Pp
Then
.Nm
takes the values
.Cm job
and
.Cm outputs
from the card and
.Cm return_max
from the configuration.
It reads only the header: the first line between the opening
.Ql ---
line and the next
.Ql ---
line that begins with the key, a colon, a space and a double quote.
A body line of the same shape is never read.
.Pp
The card is refused if one of its
.Cm outputs
names a file that a ustar header cannot hold: a name longer than 100 bytes
that cannot be split at one
.Ql /
into a prefix of at most 155 bytes and a name of at most 100 bytes.
It is refused if a job of the same name exists in any stage: an identity
is never reused.
Otherwise the staging directory, holding
.Pa card
and
.Pa return_max ,
is committed to
.Pa queue/ Ns Ar job .
The job keeps that
.Cm return_max ;
a later edit of
.Pa /etc/route.conf
does not change it.
.It Cm hold Ar job
Commit
.Ar job
from
.Pa queue/ ,
.Pa sent/
or
.Pa delivered/
to
.Pa held/ ,
after recording in its
.Pa held_from
file the stage it came from.
A job in any other stage, or no job, is refused.
.It Cm release Ar job
Commit
.Pa held/ Ns Ar job
to
.Pa queue/ ,
only if it was held from
.Pa queue/ .
A job held after it left the queue may have reached its seat, so it is
refused and never sent again under the same identity.
A retry is a new card with a new
.Cm job ,
made by an explicit act.
.It Cm status
Print one line per job,
.Dl Ar stage job seat
separated by single spaces, where
.Ar stage
is the name of its stage directory.
The lines are in byte order, so they are sorted by stage name and then by
job.
It changes nothing.
.It Cm serve Ar seat
Read one request from standard input and answer it for
.Ar seat ,
as the next section describes.
.El
.Ss Request protocol
A request is one line of at most 128 bytes, including its terminating
newline.
.Nm
reads it one byte at a time, so it never consumes a byte after the newline.
Its words are separated by single spaces; a line with an empty word, a NUL
byte or no newline within 128 bytes is refused.
On this channel, standard output carries only the protocol; diagnostics go
to standard error.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm next
Claim the job whose name comes first in byte order among the jobs in
.Pa queue/
whose
.Cm seat
is
.Ar seat .
Commit it to
.Pa sent/ ,
then write to standard output the line
.Dl card Ar job sha256 size
followed by exactly
.Ar size
bytes, the card.
.Ar sha256
is the SHA256 of those bytes in 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits and
.Ar size
is a decimal count.
If no job is queued for
.Ar seat ,
exit 3 and print nothing.
.It Cm delivered Ar job
Commit
.Pa sent/ Ns Ar job
to
.Pa delivered/ .
The seat's client sends this only after it has checked the card's digest.
It may send it again after a broken connection: for this seat's job already
in
.Pa delivered/
or
.Pa returned/
it changes nothing and exits 0.
.It Cm returned Ar job size
.Ar size
must be a canonical decimal: digits only, no sign, no leading zero, at most
67108864.
The job must be this seat's and in
.Pa delivered/
or
.Pa returned/ ,
and
.Ar size
must not exceed the job's
.Cm return_max .
If any of these fails, nothing more is read.
Otherwise read exactly
.Ar size
bytes, the return archive, and nothing more, and check it as
.Sx Return archive
describes.
If it holds and the job is in
.Pa delivered/ ,
store it byte for byte as
.Pa return.tar
in the job's directory and commit the job to
.Pa returned/ .
If the job is already in
.Pa returned/ ,
a repeat changes nothing and exits 0 when the archive has the same SHA256
as the stored one, and is refused when it does not.
.El
.Pp
Anything else is refused and changes nothing: an unknown or malformed
request, a job that does not exist or whose
.Cm seat
is not
.Ar seat ,
and a job in a stage the request does not allow.
.Ss Return archive
The archive is POSIX ustar, as
.Ql tar -cf - Ar names
on
.Ox
writes it for a list of regular files.
It must hold exactly the card's
.Cm outputs
plus the top-level file
.Pa SHA256SUMS ,
and is refused unless all of these hold:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ar size
is a multiple of 512;
.It
every header has the magic
.Ql ustar
followed by a NUL and the version
.Ql 00 ,
and a correct checksum;
.It
every entry is a regular file, of type
.Ql 0
or NUL: links, directories, devices, FIFOs, pax extended headers and GNU
long names are refused;
.It
every entry's name, its prefix field and name field joined by
.Ql / ,
is one of the expected names, and no name is repeated or missing;
.It
every entry's size lies within the archive;
.It
after the last entry's data every byte up to
.Ar size
is zero, and there are at least two zero blocks of 512 bytes;
.It
.Pa SHA256SUMS
lists exactly the outputs, never itself, one per line in any order, each as
64 lowercase hexadecimal digits, two spaces, the name and a newline;
.It
each listed digest equals the SHA256 that
.Nm
computes for that file.
.El
.Pp
An archive padded with zeros to a whole record is accepted.
The SHA256 of the stored archive goes into the ledger; a review card's
.Cm subject_sha256
binds those exact bytes.
.Ss Ledger
Each commit appends one line to
.Pa ledger
with one
.Xr write 2 ,
then calls
.Xr fsync 2 :
.Dl Ar seconds verb job from to Op Ar sha256
.Ar seconds
is the time in seconds since the epoch;
.Ar verb
is
.Cm add ,
.Cm hold ,
.Cm release ,
.Cm next ,
.Cm delivered
or
.Cm returned ;
.Ar from
and
.Ar to
are stage directory names, with
.Ql tmp
as the
.Ar from
of
.Cm add ;
.Ar sha256
follows only for
.Cm returned ,
as the archive's digest.
The same requests from the same state give the same decisions and the same
ledger, apart from the first column.
.Ss Durability
Before a commit's
.Xr rename 2 ,
every file written for it is fsynced, and so is the directory it was
written in.
After the rename, the destination and source stage directories are
fsynced, and then the ledger line is written and fsynced.
Staging before a commit, in
.Pa tmp/
or inside the job's directory, is safe to redo when a transition is retried
after a kill.
.Ss Authority
.Nm
protects the custody of cards and returns: which seat may receive a card,
that no card goes out twice, and that a return holds exactly what its card
allows.
It trusts
.Xr card 1 ,
.Pa /etc/route.conf ,
the operator, and
.Xr sshd 8
to bind each channel to its seat; it trusts nothing a seat sends.
Before any untrusted byte is read it confines itself with
.Xr unveil 2
and
.Xr pledge 2 .
Every verb sees only the state directory, read-only for
.Cm status .
.Cm add
also sees its
.Ar file
and
.Pa /etc/route.conf
read-only, and
.Pa /usr/local/bin/card
for execution only; it alone may fork and exec, and it runs
.Xr card 1
with an empty environment and only descriptors 0 to 2.
The other verbs never exec.
A seat's authority is its key in the router user's
.Pa authorized_keys ;
the operator grants it by adding the forced-command key and revokes it by
removing that key.
A failure exits with a diagnostic and commits nothing partial; see
.Sx CAVEATS
for what a kill or power loss leaves.
.Sh FILES
.Nm
never creates this layout; the install does.
If a stage directory,
.Pa tmp/ ,
.Pa ledger
or
.Pa lock
is missing,
.Nm
exits 2.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/route/
The state directory.
.It Pa /var/route/queue/
Jobs waiting for their seat.
.It Pa /var/route/sent/
Jobs claimed with
.Cm next .
.It Pa /var/route/delivered/
Jobs whose seat confirmed delivery.
.It Pa /var/route/returned/
Jobs whose return was accepted.
.It Pa /var/route/held/
Jobs the operator held.
.It Pa /var/route/tmp/
Staging for
.Cm add ,
on the same file system as the stage directories.
.It Pa /var/route/ledger
The history, opened for appending only.
.It Pa /var/route/lock
The file locked with
.Xr flock 2 .
.It Pa /var/route/ Ns Ar stage Ns Pa / Ns Ar job Ns Pa /card
The exact bytes accepted by
.Cm add .
.It Pa /var/route/ Ns Ar stage Ns Pa / Ns Ar job Ns Pa /return_max
The
.Cm return_max
in force when the job was added, in decimal, and a newline.
.It Pa /var/route/ Ns Ar stage Ns Pa / Ns Ar job Ns Pa /held_from
The stage the job was last held from, and a newline.
.It Pa /var/route/ Ns Ar stage Ns Pa / Ns Ar job Ns Pa /return.tar
In
.Pa returned/ ,
the exact accepted archive.
In
.Pa delivered/
it can only be staging left by an interrupted return; it is rewritten by
the next one.
.It Pa /etc/route.conf
The operator's parameters; see
.Xr route.conf 5 .
.It Pa /usr/local/bin/card
The validator run by
.Cm add .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Done, including a repeated
.Cm delivered
or
.Cm returned
that changed nothing.
.It 1
Refused: a bad request or operand, the wrong seat, the wrong stage, a card
refused by
.Xr card 1
or by the checks above, or a failed return check.
Nothing changed.
.It 2
A usage error, a missing layout, unreadable configuration or state, a
failure to run
.Xr card 1 ,
or an I/O error.
.It 3
Nothing to do:
.Cm next
found no job queued for this seat.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Make a state directory for tests:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mkdir st st/queue st/sent st/delivered st/returned st/held st/tmp
$ touch st/ledger st/lock
.Ed
.Pp
Queue the example card of
.Xr jobcard 5
and list the state:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ route -d st add J001-PORT-CORE.card
$ route -d st status
queue J001-PORT-CORE alpha
.Ed
.Pp
A forced-command line in the router user's
.Pa authorized_keys
binds a key to seat
.Ql alpha :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
restrict,command="/usr/local/sbin/route serve alpha" ssh-ed25519 AAAA...
.Ed
.Pp
The same requests, without
.Xr ssh 1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo next | route -d st serve alpha > got
$ head -n 1 got | cut -d ' ' -f 1,2
card J001-PORT-CORE
$ echo delivered J001-PORT-CORE | route -d st serve alpha
.Ed
.Pp
Build and return the work:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ for f in core/core.c core/Makefile RETURN.md; do
>	printf '%s  %s\en' "$(sha256 -q "$f")" "$f"; done > SHA256SUMS
$ tar -cf ret.tar SHA256SUMS core/core.c core/Makefile RETURN.md
$ { echo "returned J001-PORT-CORE $(wc -c < ret.tar | tr -d ' ')";
>	cat ret.tar; } | route -d st serve alpha
.Ed
.Pp
Had the seat never confirmed delivery, the operator could hold the job
instead.
It is never released to the queue again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ route -d st hold J001-PORT-CORE
$ route -d st release J001-PORT-CORE
route: J001-PORT-CORE: held after leaving the queue; a retry is a new card
$ echo $?
1
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Each diagnostic is one line on standard error that begins with
.Ql route:\ \& ,
names the reason and, for an I/O error, the file and the system error.
.Xr card 1
writes its own diagnostics before
.Nm
refuses its card.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr card 1 ,
.Xr tar 1 ,
.Xr flock 2 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr jobcard 5 ,
.Xr route.conf 5 ,
.Xr sshd 8
.Sh AUTHORS
Written by Claude (claude.ai/code), started by Claudia for Hugh, for plan
goal SPINE-3.
.Sh CAVEATS
The ledger may lack its last line.
A kill, or a failed directory or ledger write, after a commit's
.Xr rename 2
and before its ledger line leaves consistent state with that one line
missing.
The stage directories remain the truth.
.Pp
Nothing is ever resent.
A job in
.Pa sent/
that is never confirmed delivered stays there;
.Cm status
shows it and the operator decides.
To try again, the operator holds the job and adds a new card with a new
.Cm job .
.Pp
After a power loss, every transition for which
.Nm
had exited 0 survives with its ledger line, provided the file system
honours
.Xr fsync 2
on files and directories.
A commit whose rename completed may survive without its ledger line.
Staging that was not yet committed may survive, whole or in part, in
.Pa tmp/
or as
.Pa return.tar
or
.Pa held_from
inside a job's directory; it is never taken as committed.
.Pp
The faults for the proofs exist only in a separate test build,
.Pa route-test ,
made with
.Ql make test-build ,
which compiles with
.Fl DROUTE_TEST .
There, if the environment variable
.Ev ROUTE_FAULT
names a verb
.Pf ( Cm add ,
.Cm hold ,
.Cm release ,
.Cm next ,
.Cm delivered
or
.Cm returned ) ,
that verb kills itself with
.Dv SIGKILL
after its rename and directory fsyncs and before its ledger line;
.Ev ROUTE_CONF
and
.Ev ROUTE_CARD
replace
.Pa /etc/route.conf
and
.Pa /usr/local/bin/card .
The installed
.Nm
contains none of this and ignores its environment.
.Pp
.Xr jobcard 5
allows output names that a ustar header cannot hold.
.Cm add
refuses such cards, since their jobs could never be returned.
Tightening the grammar is debt for the next schema version of
.Xr jobcard 5 ,
owned by claudia.
.Pp
The state only grows.
Nothing is ever removed: not jobs, not ledger lines, not the
.Pa held_from
file after a release, and not the staging directory that a refused or
interrupted
.Cm add
leaves in
.Pa tmp/ .
The operator archives the state outside
.Nm .
.Pp
A
.Cm returned
request reads and checks its archive before it takes the lock, so a slow
upload holds up no other channel; the stage is checked again under the
lock.
